    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-citizenship-act-2007-sec-2a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "2A Simplified outline",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-citizenship-act-2007#sec-2a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-citizenship-act-2007.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-citizenship-act-2007.pdf, p.8",
      "values:originalText": "The following is a simplified outline of this Act:\nWhat this Act covers\nThis Act sets out how you become an Australian citizen, the\ncircumstances in which you may cease to be a citizen and some\nother matters related to citizenship.\nBecoming an Australian citizen\nThere are a range of ways you can become an Australian citizen.\n\n\nPreliminary  Part 1\n\nSection 2A\n\nAustralian Citizenship Act 2007\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nAcquiring citizenship automatically\nGenerally, you become an Australian citizen automatically if you\nare born in Australia and one or both of your parents are Australian\ncitizens or permanent residents when you are born.\nThere are some other, less common, ways of automatically\nbecoming a citizen.\nDivision 1 of Part 2 has details about acquiring citizenship\nautomatically.\nAlso, if you were a citizen under the old Act immediately before\nthe day that this section commences, you will continue to be a\ncitizen: see subsection 4(1).\nAcquiring citizenship by application\nThe other way to become an Australian citizen is to apply to the\nMinister. This is covered by Division 2 of Part 2. There are\n4 situations in which you can apply for citizenship.\nThe first is citizenship by descent. Generally, you would apply for\nthis if you were born outside Australia and one or both of your\nparents were Australian citizens when you were born. Citizenship\nby descent is covered by Subdivision A.\nThe second is citizenship for persons adopted in accordance with\nthe Hague Convention on Intercountry Adoption or a bilateral\narrangement: see Subdivision AA.\nThe third is citizenship by conferral. Generally, you would need to\nbe a permanent resident and willing to make a pledge of\ncommitment to apply for citizenship by conferral. You may need to\nsuccessfully complete a citizenship test. There are some less\ncommon circumstances in which you can apply for citizenship by\nconferral. Citizenship by conferral is covered by Subdivision B.",
      "skos:definition": "The following is a simplified outline of this Act:\nWhat this Act covers\nThis Act sets out how you become an Australian citizen, the\ncircumstances in which you may cease to be a citizen and some\nother matters related to citizenship.\nBecoming an Australian citizen\nThere are a range of ways you can become an Australian citizen.\n\n\nPreliminary  Part 1\n\nSection 2A\n\nAustralian Citizenship Act 2007\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nAcquiring citizenship automatically\nGenerally, you become an Australian citizen automatically if you\nare born in Australia and one or both of your parents are Australian\ncitizens or permanent residents when you are born.\nThere are some other, less common, ways of automatically\nbecoming a citizen.\nDivision 1 of Part 2 has details about acquiring citizenship\nautomatically.\nAlso, if you were a citizen under the old Act immediately before\nthe day that this section commences, you will continue to be a\ncitizen: see subsection 4(1).\nAcquiring citizenship by application\nThe other way to become an Australian citizen is to apply to the\nMinister. This is covered by Division 2 of Part 2. There are\n4 situations in which you can apply for citizenship.\nThe first is citizenship by descent. Generally, you would apply for\nthis if you were born outside Australia and one or both of your\nparents were Australian citizens when you were born. Citizenship\nby descent is covered by Subdivision A.\nThe second is citizenship for persons adopted in accordance with\nthe Hague Convention on Intercountry Adoption or a bilateral\narrangement: see Subdivision AA.\nThe third is citizenship by conferral. Generally, you would need to\nbe a permanent resident and willing to make a pledge of\ncommitment to apply for citizenship by conferral. You may need to\nsuccessfully complete a citizenship test. There are some less\ncommon circumstances in which you can apply for citizenship by\nconferral. Citizenship by conferral is covered by Subdivision B.",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-citizenship-act-2007-sec-2a-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-citizenship-act-2007-sec-2a-logic-deontic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-citizenship-act-2007-sec-2a-logic-alleninterval-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-citizenship-act-2007-sec-2a-logic-deontic-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-citizenship-act-2007-sec-2a-logic-deontic-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "d995c7da238a2abcaa7cfa3f5786deab395959b0efb396f52b2e44b26dd3063f",
      "skos:note": "You become an Australian citizen automatically if you are born in Australia and one or both of your parents are Australian citizens or permanent residents when you are born."
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-citizenship-act-2007#sec-2a",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "2A Simplified outline",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 8,
      "values:originalText": "The following is a simplified outline of this Act:\nWhat this Act covers\nThis Act sets out how you become an Australian citizen, the\ncircumstances in which you may cease to be a citizen and some\nother matters related to citizenship.\nBecoming an Australian citizen\nThere are a range of ways you can become an Australian citizen.\n\n\nPreliminary  Part 1\n\nSection 2A\n\nAustralian Citizenship Act 2007\n\nCompilation date: 04/06/2026\n\nAcquiring citizenship automatically\nGenerally, you become an Australian citizen automatically if you\nare born in Australia and one or both of your parents are Australian\ncitizens or permanent residents when you are born.\nThere are some other, less common, ways of automatically\nbecoming a citizen.\nDivision 1 of Part 2 has details about acquiring citizenship\nautomatically.\nAlso, if you were a citizen under the old Act immediately before\nthe day that this section commences, you will continue to be a\ncitizen: see subsection 4(1).\nAcquiring citizenship by application\nThe other way to become an Australian citizen is to apply to the\nMinister. This is covered by Division 2 of Part 2. There are\n4 situations in which you can apply for citizenship.\nThe first is citizenship by descent. Generally, you would apply for\nthis if you were born outside Australia and one or both of your\nparents were Australian citizens when you were born. Citizenship\nby descent is covered by Subdivision A.\nThe second is citizenship for persons adopted in accordance with\nthe Hague Convention on Intercountry Adoption or a bilateral\narrangement: see Subdivision AA.\nThe third is citizenship by conferral. Generally, you would need to\nbe a permanent resident and willing to make a pledge of\ncommitment to apply for citizenship by conferral. You may need to\nsuccessfully complete a citizenship test. There are some less\ncommon circumstances in which you can apply for citizenship by\nconferral. Citizenship by conferral is covered by Subdivision B."
    },
    {
